 ## Overview
-Two-Pass Surfel Rendering with Enlarged Splat is a technique for rendering point-cloud-derived surfaces (surfels) that resolves the sharp square-shaped boundary artifacts inherent in standard alpha-blended splatting [S1, unofficial]. It separates visibility determination from shading into two distinct rendering passes, allowing the splat footprint to be enlarged in the second pass to cover surface holes without causing distant surfels to incorrectly occlude closer ones [S1, unofficial]. This method is specifically designed for hermetic surfaces, such as scanned 4D media, where view-dependent sorting is too expensive [S1, unofficial].
